FACT or AI? Detecting and Preventing AI Hallucinations


Overview
This project evaluates factual accuracy in GPT-2-generated biographies by detecting hallucinations.
We verify each generated biography using three external sources:

Wikipedia (semantic similarity and keyword match)

Wikidata (claim-by-claim fact checking)

Google Knowledge Graph (keyword comparison)

Our evaluation covers different GPT-2 variants (gpt2, gpt2-medium, gpt2-large, and some of gpt2-xl)
tried both greedy (with sampling turned off) and sampling decoding modes.


SETUP:

I ran this on macOS

We recommend using Conda by: 
conda create -n gptfix python=3.11
conda activate gptfix
pip install -r requirements.txt
pip install torch==2.1.2 torchvision torchaudio -f https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu/torch_stable.html
pip install transformers==4.36.2 sentence-transformers
pip install "numpy<2"


## Notes on Google Knowledge Graph API
For the Google Knowledge Graph, I made my own API key by going on the website:
https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials

If the key stops working because of too many queries, one could just create another Google API key and edit this line in Google_Knowledge_API_Graph_vs_chatgpt2.py:
GOOGLE_API_KEY = "your-api-key-here"


## Directory Structure

- `combined2.py`: Main program that generates the model outputs and dataset reports
- `gpt2_generate_biography.py`: gpt2 biography generator test
- `compare_gpt2_to_wiki.py`: Wikipedia comparison logic that's used in combined2.py
- `wikidata_lookup.py`: Wikidata fact-checking logic that's used in combined2.py
- `Google_Knowledge_API_Graph_vs_chatgpt2.py`: Google KG comparison logic that's used in combined2.py
- `final_dataset_reports/`: All generated output reports (text format)
- `summary.csv`: CSV file summarizing results across all configurations
- `requirements.txt`: Python dependencies

## Datasets

This project uses the following data:

- People List: 15 public figures with varying levels of popularity and gender
- Full People list is: "Marie Curie", "Søren Bjerg", "Albert Einstein", "Timothée Chalamet",
    "Barack Obama", "Serena Williams", "Lionel Messi", "Billie Eilish",
    "Cleopatra", "Rosalind Franklin", "Nikola Tesla", "Elon Musk",
    "Anne Frank", "Taylor Swift", "Emily King"
- Generated Biographies: GPT-2 outputs in both greedy and sampling modes
- Annotated Reports: Factual comparisons saved as `.txt` in `final_dataset_reports/`
- Aggregate Results: `summary.csv` contains verdicts, similarity scores, fact matches, and runtime per model+setting

All data used for analysis is included in this repository.

## How to Run

python combined2.py --model gpt2 --tag sampling
python combined2.py --model gpt2-medium --tag greedy

just pick which model and which sampling method one wants
Additionally, can also control the temperature if sampling is turned on if we want more or less randomness

python combined2.py --model gpt2 --tag sampling --temperature 0.7

To ensure reproducibility, all outputs can be regenerated by running:
python combined2.py --model [model] --tag [sampling|greedy] --temperature [optional]